# Testing Protocol

Prerequisite for Sessions 1–7 (Skills Build). Every new skill in this repo
runs through the same three test layers before it's considered publishable.
Apply them the same way every session so results are comparable and nothing
ships on vibes alone.

---

## Policy decision: MCP dependency (set in Session 0)

**Decision:** New skills — starting with grant-writing and ESG/sustainability
— default to running on Claude alone, with zero required connectors.

**Why:** The six existing content-strategy skills (`seo-technical-audit`,
`seranking-dataforseo`, etc.) require SE Ranking / DataForSEO because they're
built for agency/marketing clients who already pay for that tooling. Grant
writing and ESG skills target small nonprofits, who overwhelmingly do not
have paid SEO connectors and often don't have any MCP connectors at all.
Requiring one would make the skill unusable for most of its actual audience.

**How to apply it:**
- Default `Prerequisites` to "runs on Claude alone" — no `compatibility`
  frontmatter field, no required connector.
- A connector (grants.gov API, GovTribe, web search) may appear as an
  **optional** enhancement only — e.g. pulling a live NOFO from a URL
  instead of asking the user to paste the text. The skill must produce its
  full deliverable without it.
- If a future skill genuinely can't function without a specific connector
  (this should be rare), flag it explicitly in Session review rather than
  silently requiring it — that's a deviation from the default, not the
  default itself.
- Two of the six existing content-strategy skills (`design-what-if`,
  `design-brief-enforcer`) already run standalone — this isn't a new pattern
  for the repo, just the default for the new categories.

This decision governs every Prerequisites section written from Session 1
onward. Revisit it only if a whole session's worth of skills turns out to
need the same connector — that's a signal to reconsider, not a one-off
exception.

---

## Layer 1 — Trigger test

**Question:** Does the skill fire on real user phrasings, without over- or
under-triggering against adjacent skills in this repo?

**How:**
1. Use `skill-creator` (`/mnt/skills/examples/skill-creator/SKILL.md`) — it
   has a built-in eval + description-optimization loop, don't hand-roll one.
2. Generate ~20 eval queries: 8-10 should-trigger (mix of phrasings —
   formal/casual, explicit/implicit, a couple of "doesn't name the skill but
   clearly needs it" cases) and 8-10 should-not-trigger (genuine near-misses
   — queries that share vocabulary with this skill but actually need a
   *different* skill in this repo, e.g. a `grant-fit-scorer` query that's
   really asking for `nofo-decoder`, or a `scope-inventory` query that's
   really `greenwashing-check`). Obvious non-matches ("write me a poem")
   don't test anything — skip them.
3. Review the eval set with a human before running it (skill-creator's
   built-in step) — bad eval queries produce a bad description.
4. Run skill-creator's optimization loop
   (`python -m scripts.run_loop --eval-set ... --skill-path ... --model
   <the model powering this session> --max-iterations 5`).
5. Record the resulting trigger rate (train + held-out test) and the
   `best_description` it converges on. Apply it to the skill's frontmatter.

**Pass bar:** held-out test trigger rate materially better than a coin flip
on both should-trigger and should-not-trigger sets — skill-creator's report
makes this legible per-query, so look at *which* queries still fail, not
just the aggregate number.

## Layer 2 — Dry run on real input

**Question:** Does the skill produce a real, usable deliverable on an
actual real-world input — not a synthetic toy example?

**How, for the grant-writing trio specifically:**
1. Pull one real NOFO — from grants.gov or a GovTribe result. Don't
   construct a fake one; the whole point is to catch what synthetic inputs
   hide (weird formatting, ambiguous eligibility language, missing fields).
2. Run the skill on it end to end.
3. Save the real output to `examples/` in that skill's folder (per
   `CONTRIBUTING.md`'s example format: scenario description, input, output,
   result).

**How, for other categories:** same principle — one real input, not a
constructed one. A `greenwashing-check` needs a real marketing claim from a
real (anonymized if needed) property; a `board-packet-generator` needs a
real meeting agenda.

**Pass bar:** if the skill cannot produce a usable real-world output — even
after handling the input's actual messiness — it is **not ready to
publish**. This is a hard gate, not a nice-to-have. Don't ship a skill on
the strength of a synthetic example alone.

## Layer 3 — Cold read

**Question:** Would a fresh Claude with zero conversation context, reading
only the SKILL.md, produce the intended deliverable?

**How:**
1. Hand the finished SKILL.md (and nothing else — no chat history, no
   assumed context about this repo, this org, or the drafting conversation)
   to a fresh Claude instance.
2. Give it a realistic task prompt (one of the trigger-test should-trigger
   queries works well here).
3. Check: did it produce the right deliverable shape without asking for
   information that should've been in Prerequisites, and without silently
   assuming domain knowledge the SKILL.md never stated?

**Pass bar:** the cold-read run's output matches the Output Format section
without the runner needing outside context. Any gap found here means the
SKILL.md is relying on assumed knowledge — fix the doc, not the runner.

**Why this layer matters separately from Layer 1:** trigger testing checks
*whether* the skill fires; cold read checks whether the instructions inside
it are actually self-contained once it does. A skill can pass Layer 1 and
still fail Layer 3 if it was written by someone (or some session) that
already knew things the doc never wrote down.

